    sections: [
      {
        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Solo',
            subtitle: 'Card Game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "German Solo (often just called Solo) is a trick-taking game for 4 to 6 players, descended from l'Hombre. It is played with a 32-card pack (A, K, Q, J, 10, 9, 8, 7 in each suit). Players bid for the right to choose trump and to play alone or with a called partner. The highest trump is always the Queen of Clubs, second is the 7 of trump, third is the Queen of Spades. The game survives in parts of the USA though little played in Germany today.",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "German Solo originated in Germany as a simplified version of l'Hombre (Ombre). American sources sometimes call it Modern Ombre. The fixed top trumps (Queen of Clubs, 7 of trump, Queen of Spades) show the Hombre lineage. In the late 18th century a three-hand variant was known as German Ombre (Dütsch Lumber) in northern Germany. The game is little played in Germany today but still played in parts of the USA.",
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Four players (standard); five or six with dealer sitting out or buying a hand.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '32 cards: A, K, Q, J, 10, 9, 8, 7 in each suit. For six players the 8 of hearts and 8 of diamonds are removed; 30 cards remain, 5 per player.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Standard playing cards; optionally chips or money for scoring.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Deal 8 cards each (4 players). A 3-2-3 or similar pattern; dealing starts to dealer's left. Dealer must verify each player has 8 cards before turning cards or a misdeal may be called. Deal rotates left after each hand.",
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
    ],
    description: "German Solo (often just called Solo) is a trick-taking game for 4 to 6 players, descended from l'Hombre. It is played with a 32-card pack (A, K, Q, J, 10, 9, 8, 7 in each suit). Players bid for the right to choose trump and to play alone or with a called partner. The highest trump is always the Queen of Clubs, second is the 7 of trump, third is the Queen of Spades. The game survives in parts of the USA though little played in Germany today.",
